<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      PB-DS-1 and PB-DS-4 (S22b): the spacing scale and the shape scale.

      THE DESIGN HAS NO SPACING SCALE. Substrate declares 14 distinct spacing literals across
      the phone, mode 12px, tightest rhythm a 7px gap; every one of them is a value typed at a
      call site. A 4dp grid was rejected because it turns every 7px gap into 8dp and every card
      padding into 12dp, loosening the whole layout by about 10 percent. The decision is a 2dp
      grid of ten steps, and it costs at most 1dp of drift on this artifact:

          5 to 4, 7 to 8, 9 to 8, 11 to 10, 13 to 12, 15 to 14

      Six of the fourteen values move, each by 1dp. android/gate/s22b_spacing_test.go computes
      that ledger from the design source on every run rather than trusting this comment, and it
      records which step absorbs which literal, because two of the movers are exactly equidistant
      and go OPPOSITE ways: 5 sits between 4 and 6 and goes down, 7 sits between 6 and 8 and goes
      up. No rounding rule produces both. The assignment is a judgement (a 7px gap is the tightest
      rhythm in the layout and tightening it further reads as a bug; a 5px dot margin is
      decoration and loses nothing), so it is recorded where it can be argued with.

      TWO STEPS ABSORB NOTHING IN SUBSTRATE and are here on purpose. 6dp exists for the mock's
      badge padding and 24dp for its pairing-scaffold padding; neither appears in the Substrate
      artifact, and 24dp is additionally the step the requirement's own ledger routes the mock's
      26px through. Deleting either would leave the scale unable to express a value the screens
      still to be built need. The gate asserts both directions, so "unused" cannot decay into
      "unjustified".

      DP, NOT PX. The constant this file replaces was PhoneSurface's `PADDING = 24`, in raw
      pixels, which renders at 8dp on a 3x handset - the entire spatial output of the app.
    -->
    <dimen name="swarm_space_2">2dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_4">4dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_6">6dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_8">8dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_10">10dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_12">12dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_14">14dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_16">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_18">18dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_space_24">24dp</dimen>

    <!--
      The frame. These are NOT scale steps and are named rather than rounded onto the grid: 54,
      76 and 74 are not values a 2dp rhythm has anything to say about, and forcing them onto it
      would move the safe area the whole app draws inside.

      They come from `.pscreen { padding: 54px 0 76px }` and `.ptabs { height: 74px }` in the
      design source, and the gate reads them from there. CSS px in the 386x812 mock is Android dp
      at 1:1; there is no scaling factor.
    -->
    <dimen name="swarm_screen_top">54dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_screen_bottom">76dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_tabbar_height">74dp</dimen>

    <!--
      The fourth frame constant, and the first one that is DERIVED rather than read: §5 of
      docs/design/substrate-components.md states toast_bottom as tabbar_height + space_18, which
      is 74 + 18 = 92. It preserves the mock's 18dp clearance over a bar Substrate shrank by 8dp,
      and the mock's own 100px literal is what the derivation replaces, because that number was
      measured against an 82dp bar this app does not have.

      IT IS NOT A SPACING STEP even though 92 is an even number of dp. The scale places things
      relative to each other; this places one overlay absolutely inside the frame, which is the
      same distinction that keeps the mock's `.ptime { left: 30px }` out of PB-DS-1's ledger. The
      three constants above are the same kind of value and are named for the same reason.
    -->
    <dimen name="swarm_toast_bottom">92dp</dimen>

    <!--
      PB-DS-4: the shape scale. Each of these IS its token in internal/design/tokens.json
      (p-card-r, p-sheet-r, p-btn-r, p-chip-r), and the gate joins them to it in both directions
      so a corner cannot be chosen by eye here.

      THERE IS NO FIFTH RADIUS, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE POINT. The token set declares a fifth,
      p-dot-r, at 4px - and it is a token whose declared value NEVER RENDERS. It is applied to
      `.pdot`, a 7x7px box: 2 x 4 is 8, which is greater than 7, so CSS clamps the corner and the
      dot is a full circle. The literal 4 is unreachable.

      So the status dot is `<shape android:shape="oval">` and there is no swarm_radius_dot for it
      to consult. The obvious thing to do is the wrong thing: transcribe the token into a dimen,
      hand it to a rectangle shape, and ship a rounded square the design does not contain. That
      is exactly what a reader who found four radii and a fifth token would do, which is why the
      reason is here rather than only in ADR-007.

      (The token is spelled without its leading dashes throughout this comment because XML
      forbids a literal double hyphen inside a comment. It is p-dot-r in tokens.json.)

      ADR-009 (O2): the four radii below moved with the skin (9/14/9/8 becomes 14/18/10/8)
      and NOT ONE OF THEM WAS EDITED HERE. Each is its token in internal/design/tokens.json and
      the gate joins them in both directions, so what happened is a value flowing through the
      pipeline rather than four corners being re-chosen by eye. The fifth token's degeneracy is
      unchanged: p-dot-r is still 4px on a 7px box, so the dot is still an oval with no radius
      resource, and the paragraph above still holds word for word.

      ADR-020 (W4, 2026-08-27): the four radii moved again with the skin (14/18/10/8 becomes
      16/20/12/10), through the same join and for the same reason, so again none of them was
      chosen here. The ten spacing steps and the frame constants above did not move: Slate's
      breathing spends different steps of the same scale rather than adding to it (ADR-020 D2).
      The fifth token is still 4px on a 7px box.
    -->
    <dimen name="swarm_radius_card">16dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_radius_sheet">20dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_radius_button">12dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="swarm_radius_chip">10dp</dimen>
</resources>
